        "id": 3520854,
        "postDate": "2026-09-04T10:08:29.180Z",
        "rawMarkdown": "if we are talking about a general solution for cell tracking (not restricted to the kaggle competition), to improve tracking, just increase the frame rate of the captured volume. Then you just need to have a good detector and nearest neighbour + linear assignment would have solved 95% of the problem. ",
        "content": "<p>if we are talking about a general solution for cell tracking (not restricted to the kaggle competition), to improve tracking, just increase the frame rate of the captured volume. Then you just need to have a good detector and nearest neighbour + linear assignment would have solved 95% of the problem. </p>",
        "replies": [
          {
            "id": 3521082,
            "postDate": "2026-09-04T19:52:17.620Z",
            "rawMarkdown": "This would work assuming most cells have similar velocity, unless I'm missing something.",
            "content": "<p>This would work assuming most cells have similar velocity, unless I'm missing something.</p>",
            "replies": [
              {
                "id": 3521134,
                "postDate": "2026-09-04T23:34:02.753Z",
                "rawMarkdown": "Imagine if you can improve the camera and capture image at say 100 fps instead of 10 fps. Then the cell move very little between each frame and in-fact they may just overlap a lot. So you can track them by nearest cell association. The trick is to capture faster than the cell moves or changes(division). This is hardware solution, which of course may be just a fancy solution ( eg due to limitation or cost. High speed camera are incredibly expensive)",
                "content": "<p>Imagine if you can improve the camera and capture image at say 100 fps instead of 10 fps. Then the cell move very little between each frame and in-fact they may just overlap a lot. So you can track them by nearest cell association. The trick is to capture faster than the cell moves or changes(division). This is hardware solution, which of course may be just a fancy solution ( eg due to limitation or cost. High speed camera are incredibly expensive)</p>",
                "parent": {},
